  "worked_solution": "Starting from the equation $l\\cdot m=0,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $y$ and then solve for $y$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l\\cdot m & =0 \\\\ -1\\cdot (y+1)+2\\cdot (-2)+y\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ -y-1-4+2y+6-3 & =0 \\\\ y-2 & =0 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y=2.$",
